Q: Canvas: Zooming and Panning A: <p>If you're okay with buttons instead of &quot;drag to pan&quot;, you can use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CanvasRenderingContext2D/translate" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>translate</code></a> method for panning. You can also use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Canvas_API/Tutorial/Transformations#Translating" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>save()</code> and <code>restore()</code></a> methods or following approach. Here's what I used in similar situation, but I've used buttons:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
window.onload = function() {
   scale = 1;
   canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;); //Replace with id of your canvas
   ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
   ctx.translate(150, 75);
   drawcanvas();
} 

function drawcanvas() {
  ctx.beginPath();
  //call to function(s) which draws necessary things
  //..
  //..
  drawarrow(ctx);
  ctx.stroke(); 
}

function clearcanvas() {
  let current_transform = ctx.getTransform();
  ctx.setTransform(1.1, 0, 0, 1.1, 0, 0);
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width*scale, canvas.height*scale);
  ctx.setTransform(current_transform);
}

function zoomin() {
  clearcanvas();
  scale = 1.1;
  ctx.scale(scale, scale);
  drawcanvas();
}

function zoomout() {
  clearcanvas();
  scale = 1.0/1.1;
  ctx.scale(scale, scale);
  drawcanvas();
}

function moveleft() {
  clearcanvas();
  ctx.translate(-10, 0);
  drawcanvas();
}

function moveright() {
  clearcanvas();
  ctx.translate(10, 0);
  drawcanvas();
}
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;canvas id=&quot;c&quot; class=&quot;container h-75&quot;  style=&quot;border: solid 1px blue&quot;&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;zoomin()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;zoomout()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;moveleft()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;&lt;-&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button onclick=&quot;moveright()&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;-&gt;&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;


</code></pre>
<p><strong>EDIT:</strong> I found better solution for panning problem, check <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38142308/canvas-drag-on-mouse-movement">this question</a> and it's accepted answer.</p>

Q: Query filters are duplicating SQL A: <p>Provided Brand details are fetched in a query at the car model level then the explicit query filter at the car model is not required.</p>
<p>For instance, the filter applies just to brand:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>modelBuilder.Entity&lt;Brand&gt;()
    .HasQueryFilter(x =&gt;
        x.ResourceId != null &amp;&amp;
        AllowedResourceIds.Contains(x.ResourceId));
</code></pre>
<p>... with no filter at the car model, then a query like:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var models = context.CarModels
   .Select(x =&gt; new {
       x.CarModelId,
       x.Name,
       BrandName = x.Brand.Name
    }).ToList();
</code></pre>
<p>... will still filter out only car models for authorized brands. This works because this will JOIN the Brand in and apply the filter. However, if you just query car models without selecting or filtering on anything relating to brand, or eager load the brand, all car models will be returned while the &quot;protection&quot; on brand is still satisfied. Not ideal since you likely intend for the Models for brands not authorized to be loaded at all even when you don't expose anything from Brand.</p>
<p>From a security/authorization perspective, since this is assessed at the brand level then a clear program flow would be to always query from the Brand down, removing the CarModels DbSet access entirely.  If I want to list car models for a specific brand or all allowed brands I do it through the Brands DbSet leveraging <code>.SelectMany()</code> to get to CarModels. Treat CarModel essentially like it's owned by Brand which is where your authorization is enforced. It keeps the query filter simple and targeted, and EF can optimize the queries for you.</p>
<p>Your solution with a query filter that extends back &quot;up&quot; to Brand does work, but incurs a potential penalty that EF is more concerned with enforcement of the filter at each and every configured level without guessing at optimizing it out where it should be already covered.</p>
